Palmetto: build and first cluster run
===================================

Start with one A100 and the 4x data. smoke.sbatch builds this project's custom
LAMMPS and runs 5500 steps of in.prod26. It requests one GPU, one MPI rank,
six CPU cores, 32 GB RAM and two hours (including compilation). Resource
requests are starting estimates, not measurements on Palmetto.

1. Clone the source and 4x data
-----------------------------

On Palmetto, choose a shared project directory visible from compute nodes:

    git clone https://github.com/ryan1w/Oil-Drop-Surfactant-on-GPU.git
    cd Oil-Drop-Surfactant-on-GPU

The repository includes the custom lammps26 source as ordinary files and the
4x dataset. It includes the GPU random-stream fix from engine commit
d12504677f. No submodule initialization is required. The scripts hash the
actual source files, build environment, executable, input and data for
provenance. The 8x and 16x project datasets are excluded from Git.

2. Confirm the module names
---------------------------

The initial defaults in env.sh follow Clemson's module documentation:
gcc/12.3.0, cuda/12.3.0, then the CUDA-aware openmpi/5.0.1. Check availability:

    module spider gcc/12.3.0
    module spider cuda/12.3.0
    module spider openmpi/5.0.1
    module spider cmake

This checkout bundles Kokkos requiring CMake >=3.22, C++20 and CUDA >=12.2.
Use a CUDA 12.x toolkit so the same toolchain supports all three GPU targets.
CUDA 13 removed V100 compilation support. Clemson's general LAMMPS example
uses an older LAMMPS and CUDA 11.8; that CUDA version cannot build this checkout.

If a CMake module is needed, set PALMETTO_CMAKE_MODULE to its installed name
and version before submitting. Other overrides are PALMETTO_GCC_MODULE,
PALMETTO_CUDA_MODULE and PALMETTO_MPI_MODULE. These environment variables are
inherited by sbatch under its default export behavior. The scripts reload the
same module choices when building and running. A changed toolchain requires a
new PALMETTO_BUILD_ROOT to avoid reusing incompatible CMake caches.

3. Submit the A100 build and smoke job
------------------------------------

From the transferred project root on Palmetto:

    sbatch cluster/palmetto/smoke.sbatch a100

If your allocation needs an account, partition or QoS, pass those site/account
values on this command. They are intentionally not guessed in the script.
The Slurm output files are palmetto-smoke-JOBID.out and .err in this directory.
Build files go under build/palmetto/a100; run outputs go under
runs/palmetto/smoke_a100_4x_JOBID. Each attempt has a separate directory and
LAMMPS failures propagate to Slurm's exit status.

After the job, substitute its numeric ID:

    sacct -j JOBID --format=JobID,State,ExitCode,Elapsed,MaxRSS

Check for SMOKE PASSED, inspect log.lammps for warnings, and review the fluid
temperature and sphere gap. The target temperature is 0.05 and initial gap is
9 in reduced units; transient fluctuations are expected. The short equilibration
is for deployment validation and must not be used for production measurements.
Expected files: log.lammps, screen.out, com.dat, restart.equil, restart.drive,
provenance.txt and checksum/build-environment records.

4. Repeat for other GPUs, then plan production
---------------------------------------------

Each GPU type has a separate build directory and Kokkos target:

    Slurm type    Kokkos target    Build directory
    v100          VOLTA70          build/palmetto/v100
    a100          AMPERE80         build/palmetto/a100
    h100          HOPPER90         build/palmetto/h100

    sbatch --gpus=v100:1 cluster/palmetto/smoke.sbatch v100
    sbatch --gpus=h100:1 cluster/palmetto/smoke.sbatch h100

The GPU flag and positional argument must match. Submit one build job per
GPU type at a time. Later invocations rebuild incrementally. Build alone in
an interactive Slurm allocation with bash cluster/palmetto/build.sh a100.

PALMETTO_PROJECT overrides the project root; PALMETTO_BUILD_ROOT overrides
the build location; PALMETTO_RUN_ROOT selects a shared output location.
PALMETTO_BOX can select 8x or 16x once that data file has also been transferred.

Once compatibility passes, run CPU/GPU statistical comparisons and a longer
pilot with the full production equilibration. Measure steps/second, memory
and queue delays on the available GPU types. Use one replica per GPU array
task for the first production campaign. Add periodic checkpoints and a
continuation input before jobs that might reach the wall-time limit: the
current production input saves only after equilibration and after the drive,
and this smoke script does not automatically resume interrupted runs.

Local validation of these scripts does not establish cluster compatibility,
scientific convergence or performance. Actual Palmetto execution is required.

